WEBVTT

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:08.133
Oh, and welcome to another bonus episode of how we made your mother that we liked called General Questions.
哦，欢迎收听我们是如何创造你母亲的另一期我们喜欢的特别节目，叫做“常规问题”。

00:00:08.133 --> 00:00:09.478
General questions.
常规问题。

00:00:09.478 --> 00:00:15.082
I'm here with my friend Craig Thomas, hello Craig, and our friend Alec Lev.
我和我的朋友克雷格·托马斯在这里，你好，克雷格，还有我们的朋友阿历克斯·莱夫。

00:00:15.082 --> 00:00:15.903
Hello Alec.
你好，阿历克斯。

00:00:15.903 --> 00:00:16.426
Hi, hi.
嗨，嗨。

00:00:16.426 --> 00:00:17.024
So many.
有好多啊。

00:00:17.024 --> 00:00:22.851
Are we loaded up with some general questions about Purple Giraffe episode two?
我们准备好回答关于《紫色长颈鹿》第二集的一些常规问题了吗？

00:00:22.851 --> 00:00:24.719
We are so many questions.
我们有好多问题。

00:00:24.719 --> 00:00:26.363
Hundreds of questions.
成百上千个问题。

00:00:26.363 --> 00:00:28.604
Keep them coming on Instagram.
请继续在Instagram上发给我们。

00:00:28.604 --> 00:00:30.322
They're really amazing.
它们真的很棒。

00:00:30.322 --> 00:00:39.811
And and thank you, by the way, to our Instagramers who really stuck to questions about purple giraffe, which is the best thing.
顺便感谢一下我们的Instagram网友，他们真的只提关于“紫色长颈鹿”的问题，这太棒了。

00:00:39.811 --> 00:00:42.724
Yeah, it needs to be about the episode.
对，必须是关于这一集的。

00:00:42.724 --> 00:00:44.816
That's the we've discovered.
这就是我们所发现的。

00:00:44.816 --> 00:00:47.730
I'm going to bunch a few together here.
我在这里要把几个问题合在一起。

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:51.241
But Josh, this is basically about you and Kobe.
但是，乔什，这基本上是关于你和寇碧的。

00:00:51.241 --> 00:00:56.994
We touched upon it on the episode, but people really want to hear about this.
我们在正片里提到过，但大家真的很想听听这个。

00:00:56.994 --> 00:01:02.971
So Raven X moon and the Super Boyle ask about this as well as many other people.
所以 Raven X moon 和 Super Boyle 问了这个问题，还有许多其他人。

00:01:02.971 --> 00:01:08.200
I'll read Raven X moons in terms of your chemistry with Kobe Smolders.
我来读一下 Raven X moon 的：关于你和寇碧·史莫德斯的默契。

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:12.310
This episode really solidifies Ted and Robin's dynamic.
这一集真正巩固了泰德和罗宾的化学反应。

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:16.907
How did you and Kobe develop that on-screen chemistry so early on?
你和寇碧是怎么在这么早期就培养出那种银幕化学反应的？

00:01:16.907 --> 00:01:18.069
What was that like?
那是什么感觉？

00:01:18.069 --> 00:01:21.458
A lot of people also asked like, what was like to kiss her?
很多人还问，比如，吻她是什么感觉？

00:01:21.458 --> 00:01:24.043
You didn't just meet her and stuff like that.
你们不仅仅是刚认识之类的。

00:01:24.043 --> 00:01:33.003
So sometimes people ask questions about being an actor that are like, I know that if you don't do it, those are the questions that would first occur to you.
所以有时人们会问一些关于做演员的问题，就好像，我知道如果你不是演员，这些问题会首先出现在你脑海里。

00:01:33.003 --> 00:01:41.390
Like, how do you remember all all those lines, but doing it, it's kind of like asking an accountant, like how do you, how do you do all that math?
比如，你怎么记住所有那些台词？但真的去做了，就有点像问一个会计，你怎么，你怎么做所有这些数学题？

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:46.269
It's like it's literally the job, like it's actually the job.
这就好比它字面意思就是这份工作，它实际上就是这份工作。

00:01:46.269 --> 00:01:59.150
So Kobe and I never once said, if you have a conversation about your chemistry, you're killing the chemistry, like it's an unspoken thing that you trust.
所以我和寇碧一次都没有说过，如果你去讨论你们之间的化学反应，你就是在扼杀这种化学反应，这是一种不需要言说、彼此信任的东西。

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:10.870
We got cast because we have a thing between us that is alchemical and mysterious and just works when we're on screen together.
我们被选中是因为我们之间有一种神奇而神秘的东西，当我们一起出现在银幕上时，它就是管用。

00:02:10.870 --> 00:02:21.710
I always felt like we were acting but I also felt like we were just truth like trying to beat get as truthfully under the characters as we could.
我总觉得我们在演戏，但我也觉得我们只是在追求真实，试图尽可能真实地贴近角色。

00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:25.789
I don't we never once had to talk about it and I don't I think that was a probe.
我不知道，我们从来没有讨论过这个，我觉得这是一种尝试。

00:02:25.789 --> 00:02:26.789
I think that was right.
我觉得那是对的。

00:02:26.789 --> 00:02:28.310
We shouldn't be talking about that.
我们不该谈论这个。

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:29.310
Great decision.
伟大的决定。

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:30.310
Yeah.
对。

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:31.310
Yeah.
对。

00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:36.110
I was like, if this isn't working, I assume they'll tell us, but it seems to be working.
我当时想，如果这不管用，我想他们会告诉我们的，但它似乎很管用。

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:40.310
I think you have to trust that you are cast well, you know?
我想你必须相信你们选角很成功，你知道吗？

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:41.310
Yeah.
对。

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:51.580
Sometimes, sometimes I actually, you know, at this stage in my career, I get offers for things, but every once in a while, if you want something and they don't, they don't think you're right for it.
有时候，有时候我甚至，你知道，在我职业生涯的这个阶段，我会收到一些邀约，但偶尔，如果你想要某个角色而他们觉得你并不合适。

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:52.580
You go in and you read for it.
你会去参加试镜。

00:02:52.580 --> 00:03:11.415
And sometimes I really like that because I want to let people know they hire the right person, like, I mean, I would prefer offers out there, anyone listening, but he's often, you want to go in and make sure that you're in a project that wants you and that you're the right guy for the role.
有时我真的很喜欢这样，因为我想让人们知道他们雇对了人，我是说，如果有直接的邀约就好了，听着的各位，但通常情况下，你会想去确保你参与的项目是真心想要你的，你是这个角色的正确人选。

00:03:11.415 --> 00:03:13.745
You're the right actor for the role.
你是这个角色的合适演员。

00:03:13.745 --> 00:03:16.852
So I just trusted that I was right for the role.
所以我只管相信我是这个角色的合适人选。

00:03:16.852 --> 00:03:19.118
I knew Kobe was right for her role.
我知道寇碧是她角色的合适人选。

00:03:19.118 --> 00:03:23.001
And I think we were just trying to be as honest as we could.
我想我们只是在尽量做到真诚。

00:03:23.001 --> 00:03:26.043
I don't think there was much that went into it.
我觉得这其中没包含多少别的东西。

00:03:26.043 --> 00:03:31.739
I mean, also kissing another actor is also feels curiously like just another day's work.
我的意思是，亲吻另一个演员也奇妙地感觉像只是又一个工作日。

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:35.580
Like, I don't mean to, like, you're really kissing.
我的意思不是说，你们真的是在亲吻。

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:43.436
Like, you're not fake kissing, but you're, you know, I adore at that time it was her new boyfriend and now her longtime husband, Terran.
你们不是在假亲，但你知道的，我当时的主演是她的新男友，也就是现在她多年的丈夫泰兰。

00:03:43.436 --> 00:03:48.519
It's like, you don't have any illusions that you're actually going through these things.
就好像，你不会有任何错觉，觉得自己真的在经历这些事情。

00:03:48.519 --> 00:03:50.021
You're really doing a job.
你其实只是在工作。

00:03:50.021 --> 00:03:55.798
And I know that sounds kind of unsexy and maybe be disappointing to people, but it's just the truth.
我知道这听起来有点不够性感，也许会让人失望，但这就是事实。

00:03:55.798 --> 00:04:00.419
You just do it and you laugh in between takes and you move on with it, you know.
你就是把它演完，镜头之间笑一笑，然后继续拍，你知道的。

00:04:00.419 --> 00:04:02.897
Here's your proof that it did in fact work.
这就是它确实奏效的证明。

00:04:02.897 --> 00:04:04.971
We milked it for nine fucking years.
我们靠它榨取了整整九年的价值。

00:04:04.971 --> 00:04:09.925
So it did the dynamic did just in case you want to tell you a funny story that I read.
所以这个动态……以防你想听我讲一个我读过的有趣故事。

00:04:09.925 --> 00:04:11.308
I read this great story.
我读到过这样一个精彩的故事。

00:04:11.308 --> 00:04:15.860
It was an interview with Emma Stone in, I don't know, Vanity Fair or something.
那是对艾玛·斯通的一次采访，在《名利场》还是什么杂志上，我记不清了。

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:17.478
And she's a younger actress.
她当时是一个比较年轻的女演员。

00:04:17.478 --> 00:04:29.620
I think it was before she had kind of had her maybe big breakthroughs or whatever, with EZA or whatever, but she was doing a movie with Jeff Bridges, and she, not Jeff Bridges, Jeff Daniels, and she had to cry.
我想那大概是在她取得重大突破之前，或者凭借《绯闻计划》之类的作品之前，当时她和杰夫·布里吉斯——不对，是杰夫·丹尼尔斯——一起拍一部电影，她必须得哭。

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:32.860
She thought she had to cry in this scene, and she couldn't cry.
她以为自己在这场戏里必须得哭，但她哭不出来。

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:34.019
She couldn't get the tears.
她眼泪就是流不下来。

00:04:34.019 --> 00:04:35.740
So he saw her afterwards, and she was devastated.
后来他看到了她，而她当时深受打击。

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:36.740
She was so upset.
她难过极了。

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:37.740
I blew it.
我搞砸了。

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:38.740
What's wrong?
怎么了？

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:39.740
I didn't do it.
我没演好。

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:40.740
I didn't cry.
我没哭出来。

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:43.899
And he said, Emma, you're not supposed to cry.
然后他说：“艾玛，不该由你来哭。”

00:04:43.899 --> 00:04:45.699
The audience is supposed to cry.
“该哭的是观众。”

00:04:45.699 --> 00:04:49.459
They're going to put some strings on the scene, and everyone will cry.
“他们会给这场戏配上弦乐，到时候每个人都会哭的。”

00:04:49.459 --> 00:04:50.459
Like, don't worry about it.
就像是，别担心。

00:04:50.459 --> 00:04:51.040
your job.
你的工作。

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:52.589
It's the audience's job.
那是观众的工作。

00:04:52.589 --> 00:04:53.089
Yeah.
是的。

00:04:53.089 --> 00:04:56.637
And I kind of feel that way about like romantic scenes.
我对浪漫戏份差不多也是这种感觉。

00:04:56.459 --> 00:05:00.543
And it's like, yeah, I throw myself into it and I'm doing my best.
就好像，是的，我全身心投入其中，我也在尽我所能。

00:05:00.543 --> 00:05:05.925
But you're the one that has the emotional response is the audience more almost than me.
但产生情感反应的那个人其实是观众，甚至比我还要多。

00:05:05.925 --> 00:05:11.616
If I hope that's not devastating to people to take away some of the mystique, but that's it.
我希望这不会打击到大家，打破一些神秘感，但事实就是如此。

00:05:11.616 --> 00:05:12.977
Hally Rewi asks Craig.
哈利·雷维问克雷格。

00:05:12.977 --> 00:05:20.834
I love how the ending bar scene of the episode rewind a date night was called back in the series finale, not much of a spoiler.
我太喜欢这一集《倒带约会之夜》结尾的酒吧戏在剧集大结局中是如何被呼应的了，这算不上什么大剧透。

00:05:20.834 --> 00:05:21.577
It's a line.
只是一句台词。

00:05:21.577 --> 00:05:23.804
The show was masterful at callbacks.
这部剧在前后呼应方面简直炉火纯青。

00:05:23.804 --> 00:05:30.300
How did you keep track of things that you reference that you were to later bring back in future episodes?
你们是怎么记住那些引用的梗，以便以后在未来的剧集里再次调用的？

00:05:32.069 --> 00:05:35.048
I think it's what Josh just said about kissing.
我觉得这就跟乔斯刚才说的接吻一样。

00:05:35.048 --> 00:05:38.470
You sort of have to not overthink it and not force it.
你多少得做到别想太多，也别强求。

00:05:39.110 --> 00:05:45.483
It comes, I think those things come to you in the moment organically, like calling back a joke with a friend in a conversation.
我觉得这些东西是在那一刻自然而然地浮现出来的，就像在对话中跟朋友抛出一个以前的梗一样。

00:05:45.483 --> 00:05:46.938
And you go, that feels right.
然后你会觉得，这样感觉对。

00:05:46.938 --> 00:05:47.540
Let's do it.
那就这么办。

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:51.405
We didn't have some file that was like, we should call all these things back.
我们可没有什么文件夹上面写着：我们应该把这些东西全都呼应一遍。

00:05:51.405 --> 00:05:52.709
Let's keep a running list.
让我们列个随时更新的清单。

00:05:52.709 --> 00:05:58.470
It's like if it came to you in the moment, because a writer's room is an improv game, right?
这就好像如果它在那个瞬间自然涌现，因为编剧室就是一场即兴表演游戏，对吧？

00:05:58.470 --> 00:06:04.328
You're in this room with all these people trying to go line by line, especially when you're punching up the script.
你和这么多人待在这个房间里，试图一句一句地推敲，尤其是当你对剧本进行润色的时候。

00:06:04.328 --> 00:06:07.384
The script is on a bunch of computer screens around a table.
剧本显示在桌子周围的一块块电脑屏幕上。

00:06:07.384 --> 00:06:09.473
You're looking at the lines all together.
大家一起看着这些台词。

00:06:09.473 --> 00:06:11.307
You're kind of riffing in character.
你们有点像是在进入角色进行即兴发挥。

00:06:11.307 --> 00:06:18.948
You're kind of trying to think of what this character might say and you need people who can kind of inhabit that and call that stuff out in real time.
你得试着去思考这个角色会说些什么，你需要那些能够真正融入其中并实时指出这些梗的人。

00:06:18.948 --> 00:06:23.126
Obviously, there's also the writing by yourself in a room part of the process too.
当然，这个流程中也包含一个人在房间里独自写作的部分。

00:06:23.126 --> 00:06:31.990
But I think those callbacks come when you're hanging out with a group of people and somebody sparks and realizes this kind of echoes this earlier thing and you bring it back.
但我觉得那些前后呼应的梗，往往是在你和一群人聚在一起时出现的，当有人灵光一现，意识到这隐秘地呼应了早期的某个情节，然后你把它带回来。

00:06:32.629 --> 00:06:36.297
I think it's again something you almost don't want to pull too many threads of it.
我觉得这也是那种你几乎不想去深究太多细节的事情。

00:06:36.297 --> 00:06:57.589
It always feels like a gift when you think of a really good callback because it's kind of a free joke because it's kind kind of, it's kind of already the joke you set up, but if the best callbacks twist it a little bit or reframe it in some way and this episode purple draft is, it's all callbacks because again, it is, it is this kind of groundhog day structure where you're kind of reliving the same party, although the party gets worse and worse and worse, but that's true.
每当你想出一个非常棒的前后呼应（callback）时，总会觉得像是一个惊喜，因为这有点像是一个免费的笑话，因为它某种程度上、某种程度上已经是你前面铺垫好的笑话了，但如果最棒的前后呼应能稍微扭转一下或者以某种方式重新构架它，而这一集“紫色长颈鹿”里，全都是前后呼应，因为同样地，它，它是一种土拨鼠之日式的结构，你有点像是在重复经历同一个派对，尽管这个派对变得越来越糟糕，但事实确实如此。

00:06:57.589 --> 00:06:58.518
Groundhog day too.
《土拨鼠之日2》。

00:06:58.518 --> 00:06:59.240
So it's great.
所以这太棒了。

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:04.293
I feel like Ted has, this may be an episode, we should do this if you're an interesting statistic.
我觉得泰德有——这可能是一集，如果我们有一个有趣的数据统计的话，我们就该这么做。

00:07:04.293 --> 00:07:11.513
Is this the, the highest rate of Ted saying the same line over and over and over and over because Ted has to say Ted says so that was Robin.
这是不是泰德反复说同一句台词频率最高的一集？因为泰德必须说，泰德说，所以那是罗宾。

00:07:11.513 --> 00:07:20.280
Ted says like there's a bunch of like repeat lines and callbacks and they get funnier and funnier the more times they happen and this episode was a great example of that.
泰德说，里面有一堆重复的台词和前后呼应，而且它们发生的次数越多，就越搞笑，这一集就是一个很好的例子。

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:24.870
And I think it's just the form the form dictated it the three parties dictated callbacks.
而且我认为这就是形式，形式决定了这一点，三个派对决定了前后呼应。

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:34.790
Would you also say Craig like you have a writer's room you also have like writer's assistants who are like jotting down everything and and keeping track like you have a kind of hive mind right?
克雷格，你也会这么说吗？比如你们有一个编剧室，你们还有像编剧助理这样的人，他们会记下所有的事情并进行跟踪，你们就像是一个集体智慧，对吧？

00:07:34.790 --> 00:07:37.430
Like that's yeah, it's bigger than just your brain.
就像那是，对，这比单纯你自己的大脑要宏大。

00:07:37.430 --> 00:08:05.334
Yeah, and that's I I feel like I can't say that enough times on this podcast just how brilliant our writing stuff was and our writer's assistance or script coordinate like everybody everybody was it was there was a mind meld it was a hive mind and people would throw stuff out and also like you guys on the stage would think of things Pam Fryman would think of stuff as a director and like what if we echo back this moment like it you have a big safety net you have a lot of layers of people who can think of those moments to and help out.
是的，我觉得在这个播客里我怎么强调都不为过，我们的编剧团队以及我们的编剧助理或剧本统筹是多么的才华横溢——就像每个人、每个人，这是一种思想的融合，这是一个集体智慧，大家会抛出各种想法，而且还有像你们在舞台上会想出各种点子，潘·弗莱曼作为导演也会想出各种点子，比如“如果我们回味一下这个时刻会怎么样”——就像你有一个巨大的安全网，你有很多层能够想到这些时刻并提供帮助的人。

00:08:05.334 --> 00:08:10.563
And everybody, there was such an immersive feeling to the creative team behind how I met your mother.
而且每个人，对于《老爸老妈浪漫史》背后的创意团队来说，有一种非常沉浸的感觉。

00:08:10.563 --> 00:08:15.533
It was really, we were all sharing the same delusion in the great, in the greatest possible way.
真的，我们所有人都在以最好、最棒的方式分享着同一种幻想。

00:08:15.533 --> 00:08:19.830
We were all like living in that universe, feeling like we were, we were part of it.
我们都像是生活在那个宇宙里，觉得自己就是其中一部分。

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:24.437
And those callbacks just come, those great moments come back organically in that setting.
